12
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:48,460
So, what is artificial intelligence?

13
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:54,800
artificial intelligence is the science and engineering of building machines that can observe a situation,

14
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:59,040
figure out a smart choice, learn from experience, and act to reach a goal.

15
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:02,620
A regular pocket calculator only follows fixed button presses.

16
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:07,090
An AI system goes further, adapting safely when the world around it changes.

17
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:09,630
To understand modern AI,

18
00:01:09,670 --> 00:01:12,790
let us walk through five major milestones in its history.

19
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:17,610
the computer pioneer Alan Turing asked a famous question: can machines think?

20
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:23,730
He proposed a practical communication test rather than guessing what happens

21
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:30,790
researchers met at Dartmouth College and officially named the field Artificial

22
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:37,150
engineers built rule based expert systems that helped doctors and factories make

23
00:01:37,190 --> 00:01:39,490
In the nineteen nineties and two thousands,

24
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:44,250
faster computers and bigger datasets allowed machine learning to overtake hand

25
00:01:44,490 --> 00:01:45,710
Since twenty twelve, deep neural networks, modern language models,

26
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:52,110
and autonomous robots have brought AI into daily engineering practice.

27
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:55,310
Engineers also group AI into three capability levels.

28
00:01:55,350 --> 00:01:57,410
The first level is Narrow AI.

29
00:01:57,450 --> 00:02:01,310
Every real AI system working in industry today is Narrow AI.

30
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:05,450
It performs one specific job very well, such as translating speech,

31
00:02:05,490 --> 00:02:08,490
spotting defects on a factory line, or steering a robot.

32
00:02:08,530 --> 00:02:11,510
The second level is Artificial General Intelligence,

33
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:16,510
a long term research goal where one system could learn any intellectual task a

34
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:18,830
The third level is Superintelligence,

35
00:02:18,870 --> 00:02:23,400
a purely theoretical idea of a system far beyond human ability in every field.

36
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:26,520
Now we enter Part Three of our lecture,

37
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:30,140
Core Theory: How Artificial Intelligence Systems Think.

38
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:30,460
Over the past seventy years,

39
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:36,520
computer scientists discovered that there is more than one way to make a machine

40
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:41,800
Understanding these different schools of thought helps you choose the right

41
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:43,900
engineering tool for every problem.

42
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:45,980
There are four main approaches you must know.

43
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:48,700
First is Symbolic AI.

44
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:51,740
Here, human engineers write clear logic rules,

45
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:53,280
such as if engine temperature exceeds ninety degrees, open the cooling valve.

46
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:58,300
Symbolic AI is transparent and easy to audit,

47
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:00,300
but it struggles with messy camera images.

48
00:03:00,420 --> 00:03:05,480
which uses probability numbers to make good guesses even when sensor data is

49
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:09,120
Third is Connectionism, better known as neural networks.

50
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:11,660
Inspired loosely by connected brain cells,

51
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:15,620
neural networks learn patterns automatically from thousands of examples.

52
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:20,560
Fourth is Modern Hybrid AI, which is how top robotics teams build systems today.

53
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:24,360
A robot uses neural networks to recognize objects in camera video,

54
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:26,580
statistical filters to track position,

55
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:30,750
and clear symbolic safety rules to guarantee it never collides with a person.

56
00:03:31,590 --> 00:03:36,830
The single most important building block in all of AI and robotics is called an

57
00:03:37,090 --> 00:03:41,710
Let us build the agent diagram step by step so every part makes complete sense.

58
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:44,650
First, look at the Environment and the Sensors.

59
00:03:44,690 --> 00:03:49,090
The environment is simply the world where the machine works,

60
00:03:49,130 --> 00:03:51,350
such as a hospital hallway, a farm field, or a website.

61
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:53,850
Sensors are the eyes and ears of the agent.

62
00:03:53,890 --> 00:03:58,730
On a physical robot, sensors include cameras, laser range finders called LiDAR,

63
00:03:58,770 --> 00:04:00,770
microphones, and battery meters.

64
00:04:00,810 --> 00:04:05,150
Watch how our diagram morphs to highlight Stage Two, the Reasoning Engine.

65
00:04:05,270 --> 00:04:10,670
sensor readings flow into the agent brain as structured data called a percept.

66
00:04:10,710 --> 00:04:12,230
Inside the reasoning engine,

67
00:04:12,270 --> 00:04:16,190
the agent asks a simple question: given what I observe right now,

68
00:04:16,230 --> 00:04:18,830
and given my goal, which action is best?

69
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:22,030
This brain might use simple rules, a search algorithm,

70
00:04:22,070 --> 00:04:24,110
or a trained machine learning model.

71
00:04:24,150 --> 00:04:27,530
Now look at Stage Three, Actuators and the Feedback Loop.

72
00:04:27,570 --> 00:04:30,830
Actuators are the hands, wheels, and voice of the agent.

73
00:04:30,870 --> 00:04:34,670
They carry out the chosen action using electric motors, grippers, brakes,

74
00:04:34,710 --> 00:04:36,450
or display screens.

75
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:40,990
When an actuator moves, the environment changes, creating fresh sensor readings.

76
00:04:41,030 --> 00:04:42,870
This continuous cycle of Sense, Think,

77
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:46,100
and Act never stops while the agent is running.

78
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:51,160
Before professional engineers write a single line of code for an intelligent system,

79
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:56,740
they define four clear items known by the word PEAS, pronounced peas.

80
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:01,520
P stands for Performance Measure: how do we score whether the agent did a good

81
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:03,340
E stands for Environment: what surroundings, obstacles,

82
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:06,820
and conditions will the agent face?

83
00:05:06,860 --> 00:05:11,680
A stands for Actuators: what physical or digital parts allow the agent to act?

84
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:16,440
And S stands for Sensors: what inputs tell the agent what is happening right now?

85
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:21,200
Let us work through Example One together: designing an autonomous electric campus

86
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:21,880
shuttle.

87
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:23,480
First, what is our Performance Measure?

88
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:27,300
Reaching the passenger stop on time, keeping passengers comfortable,

89
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:31,640
obeying speed limits,

90
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:33,640
and maintaining one hundred percent safety with zero collisions.

91
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:41,060
Campus roads, crosswalks, students walking, bicycles, rain, and changing sunlight.

92
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:42,620
Third, what are the Actuators?

93
00:05:42,660 --> 00:05:47,520
The electric drive motor, power steering, hydraulic brakes, turn signals,

94
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:48,340
and passenger cabin screen.

95
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:49,540
Fourth, what are the Sensors?

96
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:55,260
Roof mounted LiDAR scanners, stereo cameras, radar units, wheel speed encoders,

97
00:05:55,300 --> 00:05:56,780
and GPS receivers.

98
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:01,580
Notice how clean and practical this engineering checklist makes a complex

99
00:06:02,610 --> 00:06:07,450
Now let us see how mathematics helps an intelligent agent choose between

100
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:07,770
competing options.

101
00:06:07,810 --> 00:06:10,510
When a robot has two or three possible actions,

102
00:06:10,550 --> 00:06:14,050
we give each action a usefulness number called a Utility Score,

103
00:06:14,090 --> 00:06:15,650
written as capital U.

104
00:06:15,690 --> 00:06:19,790
Look at the equation on screen: U equals safety weight times Safety,

105
00:06:19,830 --> 00:06:23,930
plus speed weight times Speed, minus energy weight times Energy.

106
00:06:23,970 --> 00:06:26,210
Let us define every variable clearly.

107
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:29,930
Capital S is the safety rating of an action from zero to ten.

108
00:06:29,970 --> 00:06:33,510
Capital P is the speed or progress rating from zero to ten.

109
00:06:33,550 --> 00:06:36,930
Capital E is the battery energy cost from zero to ten.

110
00:06:36,970 --> 00:06:42,070
And the small w values are positive weights chosen by the engineer to show what

111
00:06:42,390 --> 00:06:45,190
Let us solve Worked Example Two with real numbers.

112
00:06:45,230 --> 00:06:48,870
Suppose we set safety weight to five, speed weight to three,

113
00:06:48,910 --> 00:06:52,450
and energy weight to one, because human safety comes first.

114
00:06:52,490 --> 00:06:56,810
Route A is a crowded shortcut: Safety is two, Speed is nine,

115
00:06:56,850 --> 00:06:58,470
and Energy cost is four.

116
00:06:58,510 --> 00:07:01,890
Multiplying step by step: five times two is ten,

117
00:07:01,930 --> 00:07:06,070
plus three times nine which is twenty seven, minus one times four,

118
00:07:06,110 --> 00:07:08,110
giving a total score of thirty three.

119
00:07:08,150 --> 00:07:12,750
Route B is a clear side corridor: Safety is nine, Speed is seven,

120
00:07:12,790 --> 00:07:14,430
and Energy cost is five.

121
00:07:14,470 --> 00:07:18,910
Five times nine is forty five, plus three times seven which is twenty one,

122
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:21,970
minus five, giving a total score of sixty one.

123
00:07:22,010 --> 00:07:23,870
Sixty one beats thirty three,

124
00:07:23,910 --> 00:07:27,040
so the agent automatically selects the safe clear corridor.

125
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:31,760
